<section id="dev-console">
  <div class="text-centered full-width">
    <h2>See your agents while you build</h2>
    <p class="paragraph m secondary">
      The gem ships a local dev console — every generation recorded as a
      trace with a span waterfall, token usage, and per-agent metrics,
      entirely in your development database.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid columns-2 margin-top-xl">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-heading">
        <h3 class="color-accent no-top-margin">One command</h3>
        <p class="paragraph s secondary">Add the <code>actionagent</code> gem, run its install generator, then flip local telemetry on.</p>
      </div>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>$ bundle add actionagent
$ rails generate action_agent:install
$ rails db:migrate

# config/active_agent.yml
telemetry:
  enabled: true
  local_storage: true</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-heading">
        <h3 class="color-accent no-top-margin">Everything a generation did</h3>
        <p class="paragraph s secondary">
          Open <code>/activeagents</code> and every prompt shows up as a
          trace: prompt &rarr; LLM &rarr; tool spans with timings, token
          counts (input / output / cached / thinking), provider, model and
          the agent release that produced it. Metrics roll up per agent
          over 1h, 24h or 7d.
        </p>
        <p class="paragraph s secondary">
          For production — retention, evaluations, cost estimates, team
          workspaces — point the same telemetry at
          <%= link_to "activeagents.ai", "https://activeagents.ai" %> with
          one YAML change.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
